 wordcards=[('Separate the event from the focus',"В магазин пошёл я. can emphasize the traveler in the supplied context: It was me who went to the shop. Here someone wrongly attributes the trip to another person and the speaker corrects that attribution. The event is still the speaker going to the shop. “The speaker did not go to the shop.” negates that event; “The speaker went somewhere other than the shop.” changes its destination. Those readings do not fit this affirmative sentence and stated context. The older label To the shop went I merely imitates the Russian arrangement in English; it is not the best natural paraphrase of its communicative focus."),('Read a story opening in its stated context',"Пошёл я в магазин. is used here at the beginning of a story about a past visit. “I went to the shop, and the story continues.” expresses that supplied narrative reading. “I did not go to the shop.” negates the event. “I will go to the shop after deciding to leave.” changes it to a future decision, which is not the stated past-story context. The retained source also gives a decision reading in a different context; this question does not infer one universal interpretation from the arrangement alone. Went I to the shop is the older literal arrangement label, not normal neutral English narration."),('Recognize a neutral report without inventing a contrast',"Я пошёл в магазин. is used here to report a past trip without contrasting the traveler or destination. “I went to the shop.” expresses that reading. “It was someone else, not me, who went to the shop.” changes the traveler; “I did not go to the shop.” changes the affirmation to negation. All three supplied Russian shop sentences describe the same basic speaker-and-shop event. Their use can differ with focus, intonation and discourse context; word order alone does not guarantee a unique English paraphrase in every situation."),('New and known information require context',"В ко́мнату вбежа́л ма́льчик — Into the room rushed a boy. Ма́льчик вбежа́л в ко́мнату — The boy rushed into the room. These retained examples illustrate an introduced boy versus a boy treated as known in their supplied readings. Russian does not add an English article by moving a noun. The English a/the choice also depends on context and what the listener already knows. The shop checks identify supplied contextual readings, not a rule that every reordered sentence changes who acted or where they went.")]
